<div class="eyebrow">GUIDE / TIME BASICS</div><h1>Time zones explained</h1><p class="lead">A local clock is an expression of a shared instant in a particular place.</p><p class="notice">WorldScope editorial team · Updated November 2026 · <a href="/info/methodology/">Methodology</a></p><h2>UTC and local offsets</h2><p>Coordinated Universal Time, or UTC, is the reference for civil time. A UTC offset describes the difference between UTC and a local clock. Offsets can include 30 or 45 minutes, not just whole hours. An offset alone does not identify a city: many places share one offset today but follow different rules over the year.</p><h2>Daylight saving time and dates</h2><p>Some places adjust their clocks during the year, while others do not. Rules and transition dates differ and can change. A local time may be skipped when clocks advance, or occur twice when clocks go back. When travelling across the date line, the local calendar date may also change.</p><h2>How to compare two places</h2><p>Choose a date and time, not just a current offset. WorldScope’s converter uses the IANA time-zone data available in your browser and flags ambiguous or nonexistent local times. Keep your browser and operating system updated so their time-zone data is current.</p><p><a class="button" href="/#planning">Use the converter ↗</a></p><p class="notice">Further reading: <a href="https://www.iana.org/time-zones" rel="noopener noreferrer">IANA Time Zone Database</a> and <a href="https://www.bipm.org/en/time-metrology" rel="noopener noreferrer">BIPM time metrology</a>.</p>
